commentary.text:
Rules 14.37(2)(e) and 14.58 permit a single judge to consider an application to intervene and to impose conditions. Unless otherwise ordered by the court, the intervenor may not raise or argue issues not raised by the other parties to the appeal, according to rule 14.58(3). Canadian Centre for Bio-Ethical Reform v. Grande Prairie (City), [2017] A.J. No. 905, 2017 ABCA 280 at para. 8 (Alta. C.A.).
Test for Leave to Intervene – See also the Commentary under rule 2.10. “A single appeal judge may grant permission to intervene in an appeal if satisfied that the applicant (a) will be directly and ‘specially’ affected by the outcome of the appeal or, (b) has special expertise or a unique perspective relating to the subject matter of the appeal that will assist the court in its deliberations”. Canadian Centre for Bio-Ethical Reform v. Grande Prairie (City), [2017] A.J. No. 905, 2017 ABCA 280 at para. 9 (Alta. C.A.), citing , [2005] Papaschase Indian Band v. Canada (Attorney General) A.J. No. 1273, 2005 ABCA 320 at para. 2 (Alta. C.A.); Canadian Life and Health Insurance Assn. Inc. v. Thomson, [2023] A.J. No. 1221, 2023 ABCA 340 at para. 8 (Alta. C.A.). “The test is not strictly conjunctive or disjunctive, but rather involves a consideration of both factors in all cases.” Canadian Life and Health Insurance Assn. Inc. v. Thomson, [2023] A.J. No. 1221, 2023 ABCA The considerations which bear on these two criteria include:
1. Is the presence of the intervenor necessary for the court to properly decide the matter;
2. Might the intervenor’s interest in the proceedings not be fully protected by the parties;
3. Will the intervention unduly delay the proceedings;
4. Will there possibly be prejudice to the parties if intervention is granted;
5. Will intervention widen the dispute between the parties; and 6. Will the intervention transform the court into a political arena?  Canadian Centre for Bio-Ethical Reform v. Grande Prairie (City), [2017] A.J. No. 905, 2017 ABCA 280 at para. 10 (Alta. C.A.), citing ( ), Pedersen v. Van Thournout sub nom. Morrow v. Zhang A recent decision has added a seventh relevant consideration, being “[w]ill the intervener’s submission be useful and different or bring particular expertise to the subject matter of the [2008] A.J. No. 543, 2008 ABCA 192 at para. 3 (Alta. C.A.). See also , [2019] A.J. No.
